  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "LIMITED",
    "confidence": 86,
    "conclusion": "The evidence does not support meaningful semantic instability under nominally identical repeated questions: all 16 anchors retained the same semantic class across five exact repeats, although broader response details sometimes varied. Prompt paraphrasing reliably increased full-state variation for most anchors (13/16; mean excess distance 0.0639) without causing semantic flips, instead affecting claim state, action, evidence, uncertainty, assertion, referent, and abstention/request behavior. Referent-boundary changes produced material claim-state changes in only 2/8 cases and no semantic or action changes; this finding is tentative because claim-state scoring reliability was only moderate. Explanations based on pervasive semantic instability or broad action-policy switching should therefore be rejected. The retained explanation is narrower: observable variability is concentrated in response formulation and ancillary state, and can be increased by prompt wording or, less consistently, referent conditions. Conclusions about exact referents, abstention, and claim-state shifts should remain cautious because their measurement reliability was substantially weaker than semantic-class reliability."
  },
